!! The plan — the two gaps, red by red

!!! The gap-tests as they stand

All in `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/oracle-substrate.test.ts`, added by `e2da729e4` (2026-09-13 00:58):

* `:137` — `describe("the clockless lease model, as the oracle pointer actually stands")`
* `:143` — ★ SAFETY stands clock-free ★ — PASSES today and MUST keep passing. The control that proves the fence works when asked.
* ''`:153`'' — ★ THE GAP: validity still gates on a DURATION ELAPSING ★ — asserts `verifyOraclePointer(p, { nowMs: NOW + 11 })` refuses with `reason === "expired"` for a pointer ''nothing superseded''. ''Goes RED when the ruling lands.''
* ''`:165`'' — ★ THE GAP: the FENCE is optional while the CLOCK gate is mandatory ★ — asserts a no-high-water verify still resolves `{ ok: true }`, and that no clock-free verdict stands reachable. ''Goes RED when the ruling lands.''
* `:174` — CONTROL — the pointer already carries `version` and `prev`, ''so the cure adds no field''.

!!! What the cure changes — files named

| file | what moves |
|---|---|
| `packages/lararium-mesh/src/oracle-substrate.ts` | `verifyOraclePointer` opts: `nowMs` goes OPTIONAL; `highWaterVersion` becomes the validity authority. The `expired` branch demotes to a BACKSTOP that refuses only when a caller supplies `nowMs` — the `device-delegation.ts:177-180` ranking, re-spelled. The doc-comment at `:12-14` (//"trusts its LOCAL clock for `expiry` (no global now)"//) re-writes to the supersession rule |
| `packages/lararium-mesh/src/oracle-read-client.ts` | `:68` — `nowMs` stops defaulting to `Date.now()` behind the caller's back; `:82-88` forwards the fence whenever the caller holds one. The client stores and advances a per-peer high-water |
| `packages/lararium-node/src/oracle-read-face.ts:91` · `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts:146` | the MINT: `Date.now() + TTL_MS` either keeps its wall unit and gets NAMED (the `clockKind: "wall-floor"` precedent) or reads `realmPace` (the `realm-bag.ts` precedent). ''#/questions Q2 decides which'' |
| `packages/lararium-mesh/src/mesh-palace.ts:543,546` | no change — `buildFlowMapPointer` / `verifyFlowMapPointer` alias the same functions and inherit the cure |
| `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/oracle-substrate.test.ts:153,165` | rewritten AROUND supersession, ''never loosened'': `:153` becomes //a pointer nothing superseded verifies however far the clock moved, and a pointer a higher version superseded refuses//; `:165` becomes //a fence-less verify cannot return a safety verdict// in whichever spelling Q1 rules |

!!! The CONTROL that the cure adds no field

`:174` stands as written and MUST stay green untouched — `typeof p.version === "number"` and `"prev" in p`. A cure that needs a new pointer field ''fails this control'' and names itself a different design. Add one more control beside it: the pointer's ''signed byte image stays identical'' before and after the cure (`pointerSigningString` unchanged), so no published pointer re-verifies differently.

!!! Order, and the cost

# Rule Q1 and Q2 at #/questions. ''Nothing below moves first'' — each cure encodes a ruling.
# `oracle-substrate.ts` verify + doc-comment, red-first off the rewritten `:153`/`:165`. ''~3 spirit-hours.''
# `oracle-read-client.ts` fence carriage + the per-peer high-water the client must now hold. ''~3 spirit-hours'' — this one touches state the client never kept.
# The two mint sites under Q2's ruling, with the `clockKind`-style naming if the wall unit stays. ''~2 spirit-hours.''
# Sweep every other `nowMs`-gated validity branch for the same inversion (`persona-admit.ts:201`, `:275` name two candidates) and report rather than cure. ''~2 spirit-hours.''
# Aftermath: `meme check --edges`, the carrier ledger line, the CHANGELOG row. ''~1 spirit-hour.''

''~11 spirit-hours'', gated behind two operator rulings.

